{
  "query": "A database engineer at mcnz.com manages a Cloud SQL for MySQL instance that serves a subscription analytics service with predominantly read-heavy traffic. During a 90 minute midday surge the application experiences elevated read latency and the team wants to improve responsiveness while keeping costs efficient. What should the engineer implement to handle the peaks and optimize both cost and performance?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure a regional High Availability primary instance with automatic failover in Cloud SQL",
      "explanation": "This setup improves availability by maintaining a standby instance in the same region but it does not add additional read capacity for the workload.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add Cloud SQL read replicas and route read-only queries to the replicas",
      "explanation": "Read replicas offload read traffic from the primary instance which can lower latency during peak periods without requiring an expensive scale up of the primary.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the database to Firestore in Datastore mode to gain serverless scaling",
      "explanation": "Moving to a NoSQL document database changes data modeling and SQL capabilities and it requires significant application changes.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Enable automatic scaling of vCPU and RAM for the Cloud SQL instance",
      "explanation": "Cloud SQL supports automatic storage increases but it does not provide automatic CPU or memory autoscaling for MySQL instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Add Cloud SQL read replicas and route read-only queries to the replicas</b>.</p><p>Using <b>read replicas</b> is the standard way to scale read-heavy MySQL workloads on Cloud SQL. Offloading read-only queries to <b>read replicas</b> reduces contention on the primary instance and improves read latency during peak periods. You can size and place <b>read replicas</b> to meet demand which helps optimize cost since you avoid permanently upsizing the primary. You should consider replication lag and direct read-after-write or strongly consistent reads to the primary when needed while using <b>read replicas</b> for analytical and cacheable queries.</p><p><i>Configure a regional High Availability primary instance with automatic failover in Cloud SQL</i> is focused on resilience rather than read scaling. HA gives you a standby in another zone and automatic failover but it does not add additional read capacity or reduce read latency during normal operation and it generally costs more.</p><p><i>Migrate the database to Firestore in Datastore mode to gain serverless scaling</i> is not appropriate because Firestore in Datastore mode is a NoSQL document database with different data models and query semantics. It is not a drop in replacement for MySQL and a migration would be complex and unnecessary for handling short periodic read surges.</p><p><i>Enable automatic scaling of vCPU and RAM for the Cloud SQL instance</i> is not viable because Cloud SQL does not provide automatic scaling of compute resources. You can change machine type but that is a manual operation and it does not distribute read load as effectively as <b>read replicas</b>. Cloud SQL offers automatic storage increase which does not address read latency.</p>",
